[OS process-family observation excerpts]
The supplied app process family was observed independently from system-wide load. At startup:
PID 2737 launcher: %CPU 22.5
PID 2746 listener: %CPU 17.3
The standard `ps %CPU` field is lifetime-average and did not mirror the application's short periodic load bursts reliably, so a separate /proc interval sampler was run and recorded in `evidence/cpu/interval.log`.

Before/After result:
- CPU_MAX_OCCUPY=10: CpuWorker repeatedly peaked at 10% and cooled down; no CPU protection violation during 45 seconds.
- CPU_MAX_OCCUPY=90: CpuWorker was allowed to climb beyond 50%; it logged `CPU Threshold Violated! (55.58%)` and the process exited around 34 seconds.

Important interpretation:
- The supplied build did not emit a literal `[WATCHDOG]` or `SIGTERM` application message. The actual protection evidence is `CPU Threshold Violated!` followed by process exit code 143.
- Exit 143 is consistent with SIGTERM at the process level, but the report does not claim a literal SIGTERM application log that was not present.
